    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran's Mineral Industries: Extraction, Processing and Water",
      "description": "Understand Iran's mineral production, the distinction between ore and refined products, and documented water-management challenges."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Several industries within one label",
        "content": "Iran's mineral production includes metallic ores and industrial minerals. The US Geological Survey lists bauxite, copper concentrates, gypsum and feldspar among its outputs. It also identifies the Ministry of Industry, Mine and Trade as the sector's permitting and oversight institution. [s1](https://www.usgs.gov/centers/national-minerals-information-center/iran)\n\nProduction statistics distinguish material weight from contained metal and separate mining from refining. These categories should not be added into a single national tonnage: successive processing stages can represent the same material more than once. The accompanying bauxite series is consequently a specific output measure, not a measure of the entire mineral economy.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "bauxite-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: bauxite production, 2020–2024",
          "dataset_id": "bauxite-production",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["bauxite-output"],
          "x_axis_label": "Reporting year",
          "y_axis_label": "Metric tonnes, gross weight",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "Bauxite output declined through 2022 and subsequently recovered. The final observation remains below the first; this series does not measure alumina or aluminum output."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A regional example: water and copper processing",
        "content": "Sarcheshmeh, in Kerman Province in southeastern Iran, illustrates why a mine must be understood together with its processing and waste facilities. A technical paper presented at Paste 2011 described water scarcity as a central consideration in a proposed expansion of the copper complex. Its authors included representatives of an equipment supplier, an engineering company and the National Iranian Copper Industries Company. Their account is valuable engineering evidence, but it is also a project-participant account. [s2](https://papers.acg.uwa.edu.au/d/1104_22_MacNamara/22_MacNamara.pdf)\n\nThe paper explains thickened-tailings disposal: equipment removes water from the fine processing residue before disposal, allowing recovered water to return to industrial use. It discusses the relationship between water recovery, disposal space and expansion design. These are connected engineering decisions rather than independent environmental measures. The paper documents contemporary designs and expectations; it does not establish their performance at the present research cutoff. [s2](https://papers.acg.uwa.edu.au/d/1104_22_MacNamara/22_MacNamara.pdf)",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "environmental-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What historical environmental studies establish",
        "content": "A separate study published in 2010 sampled water around Sarcheshmeh and its tailings-water pond. Its public abstract identifies mine waste dumps as important sources of acid mine drainage and reports that mixing with tailings decant water reduced several dissolved-metal concentrations under the studied conditions. Acid mine drainage is acidic water associated with exposed mine materials; the study concerns a particular site and treatment interaction. [s3](https://doi.org/10.3923/rjes.2010.250.260)\n\nThis finding should not become a blanket claim that tailings water is clean or that downstream contamination has been resolved. The inspected abstract does not establish current water quality, long-term treatment reliability or conditions at other Iranian mines. Those questions require dated monitoring evidence and clearly defined sampling locations.\n\nFor readers comparing mineral projects, the useful questions therefore extend beyond deposits and output. How is material processed? Where does water come from? How are residues stored and monitored? Historical engineering and environmental studies help frame those questions, while current operational evidence is needed to answer them.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
