    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's automotive industry combines vehicle manufacturing, component supply and assembly based on domestic and foreign platforms. Its development has been shaped by the major producers Iran Khodro and SAIPA, changing international partnerships and restrictions affecting access to parts and technology.",
    "tags": ["Automotive manufacturing", "Iran Khodro", "SAIPA", "Components", "Passenger cars", "Electric vehicles"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:22:18Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:22:18Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran's Automotive Industry: Manufacturers, Components and Production",
      "description": "Explore Iran's car manufacturers, international production links, annual passenger-car output and the distinction between manufacturing and assembly."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "industry-overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An industry built around vehicles and their supply chains",
        "content": "Iran's automotive industry is best understood as a production network. Iran Khodro and SAIPA are central manufacturers, but their factories depend on component suppliers, engineering capabilities and access to external technology. Historical reporting documents production based on French, Korean and Chinese platforms alongside locally developed vehicles. A national badge therefore does not, on its own, establish where a vehicle was designed or how much of its content was manufactured locally. [s2](https://www.automotivelogistics.media/supply-chain/irans-automotive-industry-left-to-rust/183690)\n\nThe field also contains distinct businesses. Passenger cars serve a different market from trucks and buses; components can be manufactured independently of final assembly; motorcycles require separate production statistics. Electric vehicles introduce another distinction between a product being announced, imported, assembled and manufactured. These categories provide the organizing questions for this section's manufacturer and technology pages.",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-partnerships",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "International partnerships and interrupted production",
        "content": "Industry reporting published in 2019 described a reversal after the opening associated with the 2015 nuclear agreement. Proposed investments by PSA and Renault were followed by renewed sanctions and disruption to supplies of parts and technology. The report also described unfinished vehicles awaiting components and financial pressure on suppliers. These observations concern that historical episode; they should not be read as a current inventory count or a description of every factory. [s2](https://www.automotivelogistics.media/supply-chain/irans-automotive-industry-left-to-rust/183690)\n\nThe broader lesson is that final assembly can remain vulnerable even when many components are produced locally. A missing imported item can prevent completion of a vehicle. Conversely, assembling imported kits can sustain measured vehicle output without demonstrating equivalent domestic design capability. Production volume and industrial depth are related questions, but they require different evidence.",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "production-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: passenger-car production, 2020–2024",
          "dataset_id": "passenger-car-production",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["passenger-cars"],
          "x_axis_label": "Gregorian calendar year",
          "y_axis_label": "Passenger cars produced",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "Passenger-car output increased from 2020 through 2022, then declined in both subsequent observations. The series measures production rather than registrations, sales or domestic value added."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-statistics",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to read the production figures",
        "content": "The Japan External Trade Organization, or JETRO, reproduces an annual vehicle-production table attributed to the International Organization of Motor Vehicle Manufacturers, commonly known as OICA. Its table distinguishes passenger cars, light commercial vehicles, trucks and buses. The chart here uses only the passenger-car column, retaining a consistent product category. [s1](https://www.jetro.go.jp/biz/areareports/2025/12940875b2aa9f23.html)\n\nJETRO separately presents company production and sales reported for Iranian calendar years. Those periods overlap Gregorian years and must not be substituted for the calendar-year national series. Sales are also a different measure from production. The distinction prevents apparent discrepancies from being mistaken for changes in industrial performance. [s1](https://www.jetro.go.jp/biz/areareports/2025/12940875b2aa9f23.html)",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "cutoff-developments",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A dated policy development",
        "content": "On October 1, 2026, the US Treasury announced additional automotive-sector sanctions measures and designations covering Iran Khodro, SAIPA, Pars Khodro and other businesses. This announcement establishes the US government's action and stated rationale. Its broader allegations about industry relationships and conduct require independent evidence before being presented as established findings. [s3](https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0643)\n\nThe announcement is not evidence of a measured subsequent fall in production. Establishing effects would require later output, procurement and company evidence. This distinction is particularly important near an editorial cutoff, when an announced restriction may be documented before its operational consequences can be assessed.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "technology-and-outlook",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What would demonstrate technological progress?",
        "content": "JETRO's 2025 overview discusses electric-vehicle adoption and charging infrastructure, indicating that the sector's development extends beyond conventional vehicle assembly. An infrastructure initiative, however, should be assessed separately from actual vehicle production and use. [s1](https://www.jetro.go.jp/biz/areareports/2025/12940875b2aa9f23.html)\n\nFor an international reader, the useful test is to follow the evidence through the chain: distinguish a prototype from serial production, a locally assembled model from a locally engineered platform, and a charging announcement from operating infrastructure. Output figures provide an essential starting point. A fuller judgment also needs comparable evidence on reliability, safety, emissions, affordability and supplier capability—areas not established by this draft's production series."
,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
